-- title:
--   Atkin–Lehner operators on the Čerednik–Drinfel'd uniformisation
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $r$ and $\bar r$ are primes with $\bar r \neq r$ (`hrr`), and $N$ is a nonzero squarefree natural number (`hN`) divisible neither by $r$ (`hrN`) nor by $\bar r$ (`hrbarN`).
--
--   **Base rings.** $\mathcal{O}$ is a characteristic-zero domain which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), $\pi$ an irreducible element of it (`hπ`), $\mathcal{O}$ is adically complete for $(\pi)$ (`hcomplete`), the quotient $\mathcal{O}/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements (`hres`), and $(r)=(\pi)$ (`hunr`); $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero fraction field of $\mathcal{O}$. Next, $Onr$ is a characteristic-zero domain and $\mathcal{O}$-algebra carrying an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra automorphism $Fr$, adically complete for the ideal generated by $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`) with that ideal maximal (`hOnr_max`), such that every element of $Onr$ satisfies some monic polynomial over $\mathcal{O}$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $Fr(x) \equiv x^{r} \pmod{\pi}$ for all $x$ (`hFr`). Finally $vdet : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb{Z}$ (target written multiplicatively) is a homomorphism with $vdet(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\pi^{n}$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal{O}$ (`hvdet`), i.e. $vdet$ is the $\pi$-adic valuation of the determinant.
--
--   **Indefinite quaternion algebra and coarse moduli.** For $a,b \in \mathbb{Q}$, the hypothesis `hB` states that $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ is indefinite ($0<a$ or $0<b$) and that, for a finite place $v$ of $\mathbb{Q}$, the completion $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b] \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{Q}_v$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ lies above $r$ or above $\bar r$; $\Lambda$ is a maximal order in it (`hΛ`). A scheme $\mathcal{X}$ with a morphism $f$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}$ and a point map `pt` assigning to each fake elliptic curve with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ data over a ring $S$ a point of $\mathcal{X}$ over the given base morphism is assumed to be a coarse moduli space (`h𝒳`): the five clauses of `IsCoarseModuli` (invariance of `pt` under isomorphism, compatibility with base change, bijectivity on geometric points over algebraically closed fields, and the universal property).
--
--   **Fine moduli with full level $n$.** An integer $n \geq 3$ (`hn`) is prime to $r$, to $\bar r$ and to $N$ (`hrn`, `hrbarn`, `hnN`); $M \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}$ with point map `ptF` is a fine moduli space for fake elliptic curves with full level-$n$ structure (`hM`, the five clauses of `IsFineModuli`), and a group $G$ acts on $M$ by automorphisms $\rho$ with labels $\chi : G \to \Lambda$ forming a level-twist action (`hρ`: the automorphisms lie over the base, they realise the twist of a full level structure by $\chi(g)$, and $\chi$ is multiplicative, surjective and injective modulo $n$). The morphism $p : M \to \mathcal{X}$ lies over $\mathcal{O}$ (`hp`), is invariant under $\rho$ (`hρp`), and carries `ptF` to `pt` after forgetting the full level structure (`hp_pt`).
--
--   **Hecke tower and Atkin–Lehner endomorphisms.** For each prime $\ell$ different from $r$ and $\bar r$ there are a scheme $\mathcal{Y}_\ell$ over $\mathcal{O}$ with point map `ptT` which is a coarse moduli space for fake elliptic curves with extra level structure at $\ell$ (`h𝒴`), and two degeneracy maps $d_0(\ell), d_1(\ell) : \mathcal{Y}_\ell \to \mathcal{X}$ over the base (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) which on points forget the $\ell$-structure (`hd₀`) and pass to the quotient by a level isogeny (`hd₁`). Two endomorphisms $a_r$ and $a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal{X}$ over $\mathcal{O}$ (`harf`, `harbarf`) induce on points the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ (`har`) and at $\bar r$ (`harbar`).
--
--   **Definite quaternion side.** For $a_1,b_1 \in \mathbb{Q}$ the hypothesis `hdef` states $a_1<0$, $b_1<0$ and that $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a_1,b_1]$ is ramified exactly at the place above $\bar r$; $\Lambda_1$ is a maximal order (`hΛ₁`), $R_1$ an Eichler order of level $N$ (`hR₁`) contained in $\Lambda_1$ (`hRΛ₁`), $n_1$ a finite idele in the prime Hecke set of $R_1$ at $r$ (`hn₁`) whose meet order with $R_1$ is Eichler of level $Nr$ (`hS₁`); $\iota_0$ is an injective $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra map $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a_1,b_1] \to M_2(K_0)$ (`hι₀`), and $v$ is a finite place containing $r$ (`hv`). The subgroup $\Gamma_t$ of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a_1,b_1]^\times$ consists exactly of the units integral for $R_1$ at every finite place other than $v$ (`hΓt`). For each prime $\ell \neq r,\bar r$ elements $s_\ell$ and $sf_\ell$ are given such that $sf_\ell$ has component $s_\ell$ at every place not above $r$ and component $1$ above $r$, the product of the diagonal idele of the scalar $\ell$ with $sf_\ell^{-1}$ lies in `levelHeckeUSet Λ₁ (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` when $\ell \mid N$ and in `primeHeckeSet (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` otherwise, and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell) = \ell$ (`hs`); the groups $\Gamma_{t,\ell}$ are defined by $\Gamma_{t,\ell} = \Gamma_t \cap s_\ell \Gamma_t s_\ell^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`). The unit $\bar w$ has reduced norm $\bar r$ and normalises $\Gamma_t$ (`hwbar`).
--
--   **Special fibre data.** The order $\Lambda$ contains all rational integers (`hΛℤ`) and `coord` is an order coordinate system for $\Lambda$ at $r$ (`hcoord`: additive, unital, multiplicative in the twisted sense, injective, with dense image and the prescribed trace identity). Over $Onr/(\pi)$ there are a fake elliptic curve $A_0$, a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $X_0$ of height $4$ (`hX₀`), formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of length $2$ for $A_0$ with $A_0$ a formal module via `coord`, $X_0$, $\theta_0$ (`hA₀`), and for each $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ endomorphisms $e(\gamma)$ (over the base, `he`) and $e'(\gamma)$ with degrees `deg`. The group `hE1`–`hE4` of hypotheses (summarised here) requires: each pair $(e(\gamma), e'(\gamma))$ is a level-preserving isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg \gamma}$ (`hE1`); $e$ is multiplicative up to $r$-power multiplications (`hE1mul`); $e$ takes rational scalars to the corresponding $\Lambda$-multiplications up to $r$-powers (`hE1sc`) and conversely only scalars can have this shape (`hE1'`); for every injective ring map $E_0'$ from the centraliser of the $X_0$-actions and $X_0.\varpi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ there is $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that each $e(\gamma)$ is induced on formal coordinates by an element $\varepsilon$ of that centraliser with $E_0'(\varepsilon) = r^{k_\gamma}\, g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\, g^{-1}$ (`hE2`); each full level-$n$ structure on $A_0$ admits a labelling $\Gamma_t \to \Lambda$, multiplicative modulo $n$ and compatible with scalars modulo $n$, computing the effect of $e(\gamma)$ on that structure (`hE3`); and, over any algebraically closed field over $Onr/(\pi)$, any level-preserving isogeny pair of $r$-power degree on a pullback of $A_0$ is induced by some $e(\gamma)$ up to $r$-power multiplications (`hE4`).
--
--   **The auxiliary order $R_2$.** $R_2$ is an order (`hR₂o`) contained in $R_1$ (`hR₂`) such that every element of $R_1$ becomes an element of $R_2$ after multiplication by a power of $r$ (`hR₂r`), equipped with endomorphisms $\hat e(x)$ of $A_0$ over the base (`hê`). The group `hE5a`–`hE5e` (summarised here) requires that each $\hat e(x)$ be additive on points, commute with the $\Lambda$-action and preserve the level structure (`hE5a`), that $\hat e$ be unital (`hE5b1`), multiplicative (`hE5b2`), agree with $\Lambda$-multiplication on integers (`hE5b3`), satisfy $\hat e(\bar x)\,\hat e(x) =$ multiplication by $\mathrm{nrd}(x)$ (`hE5c`), agree with $e(\gamma)$ up to $r$-powers when $x = r^{k}\gamma$ (`hE5d`), and that the normalisation of `hE2` can be achieved simultaneously for $\Gamma_t$ and for all of $R_2$ with one and the same $g$ (`hE5e`).
--
--   **Drinfel'd moduli package.** A ring map $\iota$ from the Witt ring `Zp2 r` to $Onr$ is given, together with a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $\Phi$ over $Onr/(r)$ which is special for the induced structure map (`hΦ`) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`), a moduli package $MD$ over $Onr$ which is a Zariski sheaf (`hMD`), and a map $\eta$ sending rigidified objects to sections of $MD$, satisfying the three clauses of `hη`: on Noetherian test rings $\eta$ separates admissible rigidified objects exactly up to isomorphism, it is compatible with base change of admissible objects, and every section of $MD$ is, after localisation at the members of a finite unit-ideal family, of the form $\eta(t)$ for an admissible $t$. Further, $E_0$ is an injective ring map from the centraliser of the $\Phi$-actions and $\Phi.\varpi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ whose image is commensurable with $M_2(\mathcal{O})$ in the precise sense of `hE₀` (there is $m$ with $r^{m}A$ in the image for every integral $A$, and $r^{m}E_0(e)$ integral for every $e$).
--
--   **Identification of the Drinfel'd functor.** A family of maps $eD$ is given from the functor `ModuliPackage.G 𝒪 MD` to the product of the functor corepresented by $Onr$ with the Deligne-datum functor `Omega K₀ π`, on Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebras $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent; it is natural (`hnatD`), bijective (`hbijD`), compatible on first components with the structure map $\psi$ (`hfstD`), it translates the relation `ModuliPackage.G.IsActBy` for $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ into `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` for $g$ (`hGLD`), sends $\pi$-translates to Frobenius twists of the first component (`hPiD`), and $\pi$-translates always exist (`hPiexD`).
--
--   **Transport data.** A ring map $\kappa : Onr/(\pi) \to Onr/(r)$ compatible with the two quotient maps (`hκ`), a natural number $n_0$ and a series $\beta_0$ giving an isogeny of height $4n_0$ from $\Phi$ to the base change of $X_0$ along $\kappa$ (`hβ₀`) are given, as is a full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$.
--
--   **Atkin–Lehner quotient of the special fibre.** A fake elliptic curve $A_{0w}$ over $Onr/(\pi)$ together with $aw$, $aw'$ exhibiting it as the Atkin–Lehner quotient of $A_0$ at $\bar r$ (`hALw`) and with $bw$, $bw'$ forming a level-preserving isogeny pair of degree $r^{kw}$ back to $A_0$ (`hBSw`, `hBSwlev`, the composite $aw$ followed by $bw$ lying over the base by `habw`); the hypothesis `hJOINTw` requires that the normalisation of `hE2` can be chosen so that, in addition, the composite of $aw$ and $bw$ is induced on formal coordinates by an element $\varepsilon_w$ of the centraliser with $E_0(\varepsilon_w) = r^{k_w}\, g\, \iota_0(\bar w)\, g^{-1}$.
--
--   **The map $\Xi$.** For Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebras $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent, $\Xi$ sends rigidified curves over $B$ (a fake elliptic curve together with a rigidification relative to $A_0$) to points of `ModuliPackage.GPoint 𝒪 MD`; over connected $B$ its structure map is a Frobenius twist of $\psi$ (`hΞleg`), it is compatible with base change of rigidified curves (`hΞnat`), it is invariant under isomorphisms of rigidified curves matching the rigidification data up to $r$-power multiplications (`hΞiso`), and over connected $B$ with $r$ nilpotent it is computed by $\eta$ applied to an admissible rigidified object $t$ with $t.X$ the given formal module, transported by `IsRigTransport` with a shift $j \le 1$, at the $(-j)$-th Frobenius twist of $\psi$ (`hΞdef`).
--
--   **Normalisation and equivariance.** With $g_0 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ fixed, `heqΓ` requires that over connected $B$ a translation of a rigidification by $e(\gamma)$ correspond, for some $c \in \mathbb{Z}$, to the relation `IsActBy` for $r^{c}\, g_0\,\iota_0(\gamma)\, g_0^{-1}$ between the two $\Xi$-images, and `heqW` requires the analogous statement with $\bar w$ in place of $\gamma$ for an Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$ over a connected $B$ in which $\bar r$ is invertible, whenever the rigidifications of source and target are related by the displayed comparison data.
--
--   **The uniformisation map.** A group homomorphism $\theta_t : \Gamma_t \to G$ is given, together with maps $\Theta_f$ on $\mathcal{O}$-algebras $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent, from the triple-product functor $\bigl(\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{O}}(Onr,-) \times \Omega\bigr) \times G$ to the nilpotent points of $f_M$, satisfying: naturality (`hnat`), the $G$-equivariance $\Theta_f(x, gh)$ followed by $\rho(h)$ equals $\Theta_f(x,g)$ (`hG`), invariance under the twisted action of $\iota_0(\gamma)$ for $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ with a simultaneous translation of the $G$-component by $\theta_t(\gamma)$ (`hi'`), a unique-lifting property along surjections whose kernel squares to zero (`het`), and a complete description of the fibres of $\Theta_f$ over algebraically closed fields in terms of $\Gamma_t$, the twisted action and the Frobenius twist governed by $vdet$ (`hfib`).
--
--   **The comparison package.** The eleven hypotheses `hC0e`, `hC0u`, `hC1e`, `hC1u`, `hC1p`, `hC2`, `hC2c`, `hC3`, `hC4`, `hC4pi`, `hC5` (summarised here) govern the relation between points $(\psi,P)$ of the product functor and pairs $(E,\varrho)$ consisting of a fake elliptic curve over $B$ with a rigidification relative to $A_0$ at the $(-vdet(g_0))$-th Frobenius twist of $\psi$, normalised by the condition that $\Xi(E,\varrho)$ have that same structure map and that $eD(\Xi(E,\varrho))$ be related to $(\psi,P)$ by the twisted action of $g_0^{-1}$: existence and uniqueness of a normalised level transport of $P_0$ to a full level-$n$ structure on $E$ (`hC0e`, `hC0u`); existence, uniqueness up to isomorphism matching the rigidifications, and compatibility with base change of such normalised pairs (`hC1e`, `hC1u`, `hC1p`); the identifications of $\Theta_f((\psi,P),1)$ with `ptF` of $(E,P_n)$ (`hC2`) and of its composite with $p$ with `pt` of $E$ (`hC2c`); the transport of a normalised pair along the twisted action of $\iota_0(\gamma)$, $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$, with the resulting full level structures differing by the twist $\chi(\theta_t(\gamma))$ and the extra level structures at primes $\ell \neq r$ being matched (`hC3`); the corresponding transport along a relation `IsActBy` for $r^{c}\, g_0\,\iota_0(h)\, g_0^{-1}$ (`hC4`) and along a $\pi$-translate followed by an $r$-power action (`hC4pi`); and, over algebraically closed fields, the criterion recognising an isomorphism of two normalised pairs as coming from an element $\gamma$ of $\Gamma_t$, together with the induced identities on level structures (`hC5`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** The assertion is a conjunction of two statements, each quantified over every Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent (`hB`) and whose only idempotents are $0$ and $1$ (`hconn`).
--
--   First, for every $x = (\psi, P)$ with $\psi : Onr \to B$ an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map and $P$ a Deligne datum over $B$: the $B$-point $\Theta_f(x, 1_G)$ of $M$, followed by $p$ and then by $a_r$, equals the $B$-point $\Theta_f\bigl((\psi \circ Fr, P), 1_G\bigr)$ followed by $p$; here $\psi \circ Fr$ is `frobTwist Onr Fr 1 ψ`.
--
--   Secondly, for every pair $x, x'$ of such points related by `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct π Onr Fr vdet B (Units.map ι₀ wbar) x x'` — that is, the first component of $x'$ is the Frobenius twist of that of $x$ by $-vdet(\iota_0(\bar w))$, and the second component of $x'$ is the pullback of that of $x$ along $\iota_0(\bar w)^{-1}$ in the sense of `DeligneDatum.IsPullback` — the $B$-point $\Theta_f(x, 1_G)$, followed by $p$ and then by $a_{\bar r}$, equals $\Theta_f(x', 1_G)$ followed by $p$.
--
--   This is the Atkin–Lehner part of the Čerednik–Drinfel'd uniformisation of the Shimura curve attached to an indefinite quaternion algebra ramified exactly at $r$ and $\bar r$: on the formal neighbourhood of the fibre at $r$, the involution at $r$ is computed by the Frobenius twist of the unramified leg, and the involution at $\bar r$ by the twisted action of $\iota_0(\bar w)$ on the Deligne-datum leg. In this edition the curve-isomorphism clause `hC5` is stated over a single leg, and the conclusion is restricted to Noetherian test algebras with no nontrivial idempotents; it feeds the statement [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.cerednikDrinfeld_fineFamily_atkinLehner_of_rigidifiedToG_heightNormalised_oneLegC5`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.cerednikDrinfeld_fineFamily_atkinLehner_of_rigidifiedToG_heightNormalised_oneLegC5).
